This version uses XOR to exchange the two integers without a separate temporary variable. XOR is applied three times, which moves the original values into the other references.

The references mean the input variables themselves are changed. The same-variable case is handled before the XOR operations, because applying XOR to one variable three times would turn it into zero.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    void swap(int &a, int &b) {
        if (&a == &b) {
            return;
        }

        a ^= b;
        b ^= a;
        a ^= b;
    }
};
```

For `a = 6` and `b = 11`, the values become `11` and `6`. XOR works with negative integers too, but a temporary variable is usually easier to read. Time complexity is `O(1)` and extra space is `O(1)`.

JavaScript array destructuring keeps this solution small. The two old values are read before either variable is updated, then the assignments happen together.

A quick dry run with `a = 12` and `b = 3` changes the pair to `[3, 12]`. Equal values also work normally.

```javascript
class Solution {
    /**
     * Swaps two integers and returns them.
     *
     * @param {number} a - The first integer.
     * @param {number} b - The second integer.
     * @returns {[number, number]} The two integers, swapped.
     */
    swap(a, b) {
        [a, b] = [b, a];
        return [a, b];
    }
}
```

The method returns a new array and does not change variables outside the method. It takes `O(1)` time and `O(1)` extra space.

A temporary variable makes the exchange easy to follow. Save `a`, move `b` into `a`, then put the saved value into `b`. The method returns a new array in the swapped order.

For example, with `a = 4` and `b = 9`:
- `temporary = 4`
- `a = 9`
- `b = 4`

```java
class Solution {
    /**
     * Swaps two integers and returns them as an array.
     *
     * @param a the first integer
     * @param b the second integer
     * @return an array with the two integers swapped
     */
    public int[] swap(int a, int b) {
        int temporary = a;
        a = b;
        b = temporary;
        return new int[] {a, b};
    }
}
```

The method does not change the caller's original variables because Java passes these integer values into the method. Time complexity is `O(1)`, and the returned array uses `O(1)` space.

The Go template gives pointers to both integers, so the function can update the original values directly. A small local variable stores the first value while the pointers are reassigned.

If both pointers refer to the same integer, the swap is skipped. That avoids changing the value by copying it onto itself in the middle of the operation.

```go
func swap(a *int, b *int) {
    if a == b {
        return
    }

    temporary := *a
    *a = *b
    *b = temporary
}
```

The input integers are changed in place, and the function has no return value. The operation uses `O(1)` time and `O(1)` extra space.

## Solution
```JAVA[]
class Solution {
public int[] swap(int a, int b) {
      a=a+b;
      b= a-b;
      a=a-b; 
      return new int[]{a,b};
    }
}
```
